WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.302
Broadcasting allows NumPy to combine arrays with different but compatible shapes.

00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:09.197
In this example, sales is a three-by-two array.

00:00:09.197 --> 00:00:11.647
Each row has two values.

00:00:11.647 --> 00:00:14.867
The adjustment array contains only two values: five and ten.

00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:20.462
When we add them, NumPy applies five to the first column and ten to the second column for

00:00:20.462 --> 00:00:24.882
every row. Click Run and inspect the resulting matrix.

00:00:24.882 --> 00:00:28.722
We did not manually repeat the adjustment three times.

00:00:28.722 --> 00:00:35.617
Broadcasting lets NumPy conceptually expand the smaller array across the larger one.

00:00:35.617 --> 00:00:38.437
A scalar is an even simpler example.

00:00:38.437 --> 00:00:43.662
If you add five to an entire array, that five is applied to every element.

00:00:43.662 --> 00:00:48.592
Broadcasting is powerful, but shape mistakes can create confusing errors or even valid-looking results that

00:00:48.592 --> 00:00:52.087
are not what you intended.

00:00:52.087 --> 00:00:57.077
So when using broadcasting, always inspect the shapes of both operands and make sure the dimensions represent

00:00:57.077 --> 00:01:00.598
the relationship you expect.
